 "capsule": "By 1294, the Golden Horde — the Ulus of Jochi, descended from Chinggis Khan's eldest son — had long functioned as a fully independent khanate ruling the Pontic-Caspian steppe, the Rus' principalities and Volga Bulgaria from its capital at Sarai on the lower Volga, no longer answering to the Great Khan enthroned in China. Its power peaked under Öz Beg Khan (1312-1341), who adopted Islam as the state religion and granted Ivan I of Moscow the title of grand prince in exchange for collecting Rus' tribute, but internal strife after 1359 and Timur's devastating raids of the 1390s broke its unity. A shrunken 'Great Horde' persisted as a rump state contested by Moscow, Crimea and Lithuania until Crimean forces sacked Sarai in 1502; its last khan, Sheikh Ahmed, spent most of his remaining years in Lithuanian captivity, and the Crimean Khanate inherited the Golden Horde's claim to primacy among the Genghisid successor states.",
